CAMPUS LOST & FOUND PORTAL

PROJECT BRIEF

Create a Java console application that simulates the core behavior of a web-based
campus Lost & Found portal. Students can report items they have lost or found,
browse and search reports, and track whether a found item is still available,
has been requested by its owner, or has been resolved.

The console menu represents the actions a user could perform through a web
application. Store all data in memory using Java collections. Do not use a
database, file storage, a web framework, or a network service. Data does not
need to survive when the application exits.

BUSINESS REQUIREMENTS

1. LOST-AND-FOUND REPORTS

Each report must contain:

- ID: Unique, positive integer assigned by the application; users must not
  supply or change it.
- Report type: LOST or FOUND.
- Item name: Required, non-blank text.
- Description: Required, non-blank text describing the item; may include
  identifying details.
- Category: Required, non-blank text, such as electronics, clothing, keys, or
  books.
- Location: Required, non-blank text indicating where the item was lost or
  found.
- Date reported: Required date in YYYY-MM-DD format; it must not be in the
  future.
- Status: One of OPEN, CLAIMED, or CLOSED; new reports start as OPEN. CLAIMED
  means someone says the found item is theirs and wants to collect it.

2. MAIN MENU AND OPERATIONS

Show a menu repeatedly until the user chooses to exit. Provide these operations:

1. Add a report: Ask for its type and required details, validate them, assign a
   unique ID, save the report, and display the created report or its ID.
2. List reports: Display all reports, including their IDs and statuses. If
   there are no reports, show a clear empty-state message.
3. Get a report: Find and display a report by ID. If no report has that ID,
   explain that it was not found.
4. Search and filter reports: Allow users to find reports by item name,
   category, location, report type, or status. Text matching must be
   case-insensitive and may match part of a value. Users may combine filters.
   Clearly report when there are no matches.
5. Update a report: Find a report by ID and allow the user to update its item
   name, description, category, or location. Validate new values before
   saving. The ID and date reported cannot be changed. Report type cannot be
   changed after creation.
6. Mark a found item as requested for pickup: Find a report by ID and change
   its status from OPEN to CLAIMED. This means someone says the item is theirs
   and wants to collect it; it does not mean the item has already been picked
   up.
7. Close a report: Find an OPEN or CLAIMED report and change its status to
   CLOSED.
8. Delete a report: Find a report by ID and permanently remove it from the
   in-memory collection. Ask for confirmation before deleting.
9. Exit: End the application cleanly.

3. PICKUP REQUEST AND STATUS RULES

- Only a FOUND report with status OPEN can be marked as requested for pickup.
- A LOST report cannot be marked as requested for pickup.
- A CLAIMED or CLOSED report cannot be marked as requested for pickup again.
- Only reports with status OPEN or CLAIMED can be closed.
- A CLOSED report cannot be marked as requested for pickup or reopened.
- When a requested action is not allowed, leave the report unchanged and show
  an informative message.

4. INPUT AND ERROR HANDLING

- Reject blank required text and invalid menu choices, IDs, dates, report
  types, and statuses.
- Report IDs must be positive integers. If an ID does not exist, do not modify
  or delete any report.
- Invalid input must not terminate the application; prompt again or return to
  the menu with a clear error message.
- For deletion, proceed only after an affirmative confirmation; otherwise keep
  the report unchanged.
- Display a clear success or failure message after every operation.

TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS

- Use Java and at least one Java collection (for example, ArrayList or HashMap)
  as the in-memory store.
- Do not use a database or persist data to files.
- Represent a report with a suitable class. Keep the menu/input handling
  separate from the logic that manages reports where practical.
- Implement add, get, list, search/filter, update,
  mark-as-requested-for-pickup, close, and delete behavior; do not make menu
  options that only print placeholder messages.
- Use readable names, consistent formatting, and appropriate access modifiers.

ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

The application is complete when a user can run it and:

- Add both LOST and FOUND reports and receive unique generated IDs.
- List all reports, retrieve an existing report by ID, and receive a useful
  message for an unknown ID.
- Search using each supported field and combine filters.
- Update editable fields without changing the report ID, date, or type.
- Mark an open found report as requested for pickup, while invalid attempts
  leave report data unchanged.
- Close eligible reports and reject closing an already closed report.
- Confirm deletion to remove a report, or decline deletion to preserve it.
- Continue using the menu after invalid input and exit without an exception.

EXAMPLE

If report 12 is a FOUND report with status OPEN, marking it as requested for
pickup changes its status to CLAIMED. This means someone says the item is theirs
and wants to collect it; it does not confirm pickup. Attempting to mark it again
must leave it CLAIMED and explain that it is no longer available for a new
pickup request.